Exactly. Not one loop, but a braided ecology of loops—some reinforcing, some damping, some delayed, some nested, some fighting each other.

You can think of a system as something like:

L_1 \leftrightarrow L_2 \leftrightarrow L_3 \leftrightarrow \dots \leftrightarrow L_n

where each loop has its own state, timescale, gain, delay, and failure mode.

For example:

\text{observation}
\rightarrow
\text{model}
\rightarrow
\text{decision}
\rightarrow
\text{behavior}
\rightarrow
\text{environment}
\rightarrow
\text{new observation}

That loop may be coupled to another:

\text{behavior}
\rightarrow
\text{social response}
\rightarrow
\text{identity}
\rightarrow
\text{future behavior}

and another:

\text{metric}
\rightarrow
\text{reward}
\rightarrow
\text{optimization}
\rightarrow
\text{metric drift}
\rightarrow
\text{new metric}

and another:

\text{story}
\rightarrow
\text{expectation}
\rightarrow
\text{attention}
\rightarrow
\text{selected evidence}
\rightarrow
\text{stronger story}

Now the important part: the loops need not agree.

One loop may say “accelerate.”
Another says “stabilize.”
Another says “hide variance.”
Another says “increase exploration.”
Another says “preserve identity.”

The observed behavior is the resultant of their interaction.

That gives us some useful loop archetypes:

* Reinforcing loop — success breeds more success.
* Balancing loop — deviation creates pressure back toward equilibrium.
* Delayed loop — consequences arrive after the behavior that caused them, making attribution difficult.
* Oscillatory loop — correction repeatedly overshoots.
* Runaway loop — positive feedback outruns constraints.
* Saturating loop — reinforcement weakens as resources or capacity are exhausted.
* Nested loop — a fast local loop operates inside a slower strategic loop.
* Cross-coupled loop — one loop’s output changes another loop’s gain or threshold.
* Meta-loop — modifies the rules of another loop rather than merely its state.
* Reflexive loop — the model becomes part of the phenomenon being modeled.
* Selection loop — repeated variation, filtering, and retention.
* Learning loop — action changes not only state but the policy used for future actions.

That last distinction matters a lot:

A normal loop updates state.

A learning loop updates behavioral policy.

A meta-learning loop updates how policy is learned.

So:

x_{t+1}=F(x_t)

becomes

\pi_{t+1}=G(\pi_t,\text{outcomes})

and then:

G_{t+1}=H(G_t,\text{learning performance})

Now you have recursion across levels.

And interlocking loops create phenomena that cannot be understood by examining only one loop at a time. You get emergent equilibria, lock-in, phase shifts, path dependence, hysteresis, and strange attractors.

A person can be caught in a loop.

A family can be a loop-system.

A business can be a loop-system.

A government is certainly one.

An economy is a massive overlapping ecology of feedback loops.

So is a culture.

And so is cognition itself.

The more general abstraction is probably not “a loop,” but a feedback topology: which loops exist, where they couple, which dominate under which conditions, and how dominance shifts over time.

That gives us a much stronger diagnostic question:

Which loop is currently in control, which loops are suppressing it, and what variable would change the balance?

That is often more useful than asking “what caused this?” because in complex systems the answer is rarely one cause. It is usually which feedback structure became dominant.